<article class="page prose-page connections-page">
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How claims are connected</h1>
    <p class="lead">Claims are connected in three ways: by themes the editors set, by pattern tags, and by the bodies
      that made them. Indirect connections are then found by following the themes. A connection is a reason to read two
      checks side by side. It is never evidence for or against either claim.</p>
  </header>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#themes">Themes and direct links</a></li>
      <li><a href="#patterns">Pattern tags</a></li>
      <li><a href="#indirect">Indirect connections</a></li>
      <li><a href="#bridges">Where themes meet</a></li>
      <li><a href="#bodies">Who said it</a></li>
      <li><a href="#limits">What a connection does not mean</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <section aria-labelledby="themes">
    <h2 id="themes">Themes and direct links</h2>
    <p>A theme is something several claims have in common: a shared cause, a shared mechanism, a shared target, a shared
      flow of material, or a shared way of presenting figures. Two claims in the same theme are <strong>linked
      directly</strong>. On the map these are the coloured lines; on a claim page they are listed under
      <em>Connections</em>, with what connects them.</p>
    <p>Each theme has a strength:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Strong</strong>: the shared cause, mechanism or target is documented in the sources of the checks.</li>
      <li><strong>Weak (indicative)</strong>: a plausible connection proposed while claims are collected. It stays
        indicative until a finished report confirms it.</li>
      <li><strong>Pattern, not causal</strong>: the claims present evidence in the same way. Nothing connects them
        physically.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>{{ linked }} of {{ mizien.claims.length }} claims are linked to at least one other. A new claim has no links until
      the editors place it in a theme.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="claims theme-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Theme</th><th scope="col">Kind</th><th scope="col">Claims</th><th scope="col">What connects them</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      {%- for t in mizien.themes %}
        <tr><td><span class="route-theme" style="--t: {{ t.color }}">{{ t.name }}</span></td>
          <td>{{ t.link_type }}<br><span class="small">{{ t.strength }}</span></td>
          <td>{% for id in t.members %}{% set x = mizien.claims | findBy("id", id) %}{% if x %}{{ m.claim(x, true) }} {% endif %}{% endfor %}</td>
          <td>{{ t.description }}</td></tr>
      {%- endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="patterns">
    <h2 id="patterns">Pattern tags</h2>
    <p>A pattern tag names a recurring way a claim can mislead. It describes the statement, not the speaker, and it is
      provisional until the claim's report is finished. Claims with the same tag are listed together on each claim page.</p>
    <dl class="pattern-list">
      {%- for p in mizien.patterns %}
      <div><dt>{{ p.name }}</dt><dd>{{ p.meaning | mdInline | safe }}
        <span class="pattern-claims">{% for x in mizien.claims | tagged(p.name) %}{{ m.claim(x, true) }} {% endfor %}</span></dd></div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="indirect">
    <h2 id="indirect">Indirect connections</h2>
    <p>Two claims that are not linked directly may both be linked to a third. Following the links one step further
      shows claims <strong>two steps away</strong>; one more step gives claims <strong>three steps away</strong>. These
      are worked out by the site build, not chosen by the editors: for each claim it lists the shortest routes through
      the links, up to three routes for each claim it reaches.</p>
    <p>Each route is shown as the themes it passes through. For example, a claim about sealed soil and a claim about
      traffic emissions can meet through a third claim that belongs to both themes. {{ withSecond }} claims have at least
      one connection two steps away.</p>
    <p>The further a connection goes, the looser it is. At two steps it shows where one theme meets another; at three it
      says more about how the themes fit together than about either claim.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="bridges">
    <h2 id="bridges">Where themes meet</h2>
    <p>A claim that belongs to two themes joins them. These claims are the bridges that indirect connections pass
      through, and good places to start reading across topics.</p>
    <ul class="bridge-list">
      {%- for b in mizien.theme_bridges %}
      {%- set ta = mizien.themes | findBy("id", b.a) %}{% set tb = mizien.themes | findBy("id", b.b) %}
      <li><span class="route-theme" style="--t: {{ ta.color }}">{{ b.a_name }}</span> meets
        <span class="route-theme" style="--t: {{ tb.color }}">{{ b.b_name }}</span> in
        {% for x in b.claims %}{{ m.claim(x) }}{% if not loop.last %}{% if loop.revindex == 2 %} and {% else %}, {% endif %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}</li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="bodies">
    <h2 id="bodies">Who said it</h2>
    <p>Every claim is matched to the body or person who made it, using a register of bodies and people
      (<a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/data/bodies.csv">data/bodies.csv</a>). The register records each body's
      kind (government, agency, regulator, oversight body, party, NGO or union, business, media, EU or research body)
      and the office a person spoke for, with the role named in the source. A claim made by a minister counts for the
      minister and for their ministry.</p>
    <p>Two bodies are <strong>linked</strong> when they are named in the same claim, or when their claims share a theme.
      A link means their claims are worth reading together. It does not mean the bodies agree, disagree or act together.</p>
    <p>Each body has a page listing its claims, the topics and pattern tags they carry, and the bodies they link to.
      <strong>These pages are a record, not a score.</strong> Claims are picked because they are public and can be
      checked, not to sample anyone's statements fairly. A body with more claims has been checked more often, which says
      nothing about how accurate it is. No body or person is rated. See <a href="/bodies/">Who said it</a>.</p>
    <p>If a claim is matched to the wrong body, please <a href="{{ site.repo }}/issues/new">report it</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">What a connection does not mean</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It is not evidence. Each claim is checked on its own sources; a link to a contradicted claim does not weaken
        another claim, and a link to a supported one does not strengthen it.</li>
      <li>It is not a finding of coordination or motive. Nothing on this site says bodies acted together, or why anyone
        said something, unless documents show it.</li>
      <li>It can change. Themes are revised as checks are finished, and indirect connections follow automatically.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</article>
